## Solution

**Answer:** The minimum possible dimension is $1$ for $n \ge 2$ (and $0$ for $n = 1$).

### Proof

We minimize over all choices of a finite group $G$ with $|G|=n$ and all faithful complex representations $G \hookrightarrow \mathrm{GL}(V)$.

**Lower bound.** Suppose $\dim V = 0$. Then $V = \{0\}$ and $\mathrm{GL}(V) = \{\mathrm{id}\}$ is the trivial group. The action homomorphism $\rho \colon G \to \mathrm{GL}(V)$ has image $\{\mathrm{id}\}$, so $\ker \rho = G$. For the action to be faithful we need $\ker \rho = \{e\}$, hence $G = \{e\}$, i.e. $n = 1$. Therefore, for every $n \ge 2$, any faithful action forces $\dim V \ge 1$.

**Upper bound (achievability).** For every $n \ge 1$ there exists a cyclic group $G = \mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ of order $n$. Take $V = \mathbb{C}$ (so $\dim V = 1$) and define the action by sending a generator $g$ to multiplication by the primitive $n$-th root of unity
$$\zeta = e^{2\pi i / n} \in \mathbb{C}^{\times} = \mathrm{GL}_1(\mathbb{C}).$$
Since $\zeta$ has order exactly $n$, the homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{C}^{\times}$, $g^k \mapsto \zeta^k$, is injective. Hence the action is faithful, and it is realized on a space of dimension $1$.

**Conclusion.** Combining both bounds: for $n \ge 2$ the minimum is exactly $1$; for $n = 1$ the trivial group acts faithfully on the zero-dimensional space, giving minimum $0$.

$$\boxed{\min \dim V = \begin{cases} 0 & n = 1, \\ 1 & n \ge 2. \end{cases}}$$

